1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Okay, so let's summarize

2
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
everything we've learned about deployment on AWS.

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,000
So first CloudFormation, which is an AWS-only tool,

4
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
which is allowing you to do Infrastructure as Code

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
and works with almost all types of AWS resources.

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
This allows you to create templates,

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:17,000
and these templates can be used

8
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,000
to deploy infrastructure on AWS

9
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
and to use these templates across different Regions

10
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
and accounts to make your infrastructure truly repeatable.

11
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Then we have Beanstalk, which is, again, AWS-only,

12
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:30,000
So Elastic Beanstalk,

13
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
which is a Platform as a Service or PaaS,

14
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,000
and it's limited to certain programming languages or Docker.

15
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
You can deploy your code consistently

16
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
with a known architecture, for example,

17
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
using the combination of a load balancer, EC2 instances,

18
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
and an RDS database.

19
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,000
CodeDeploy is to deploy

20
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
and upgrade any applications onto servers,

21
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
and this can be done on AWS,

22
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:53,000
for example, your EC2 instances,

23
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
but also it can be done on your on-premises infrastructure.

24
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:58,000
And this is why it's called

25
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
a hybrid type of service.

26
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Systems Manager, yet again,

27
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,000
is a hybrid type of service,

28
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:04,000
which allows you to patch, configure,

29
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
and run commands at scale across all your servers.

30
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:09,000
So that's for all the deployment services,

31
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
but now let's talk about the developer services.

32
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:13,000
We've learned about CodeCommit,

33
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,000
which does come up in the exam,

34
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
which is to store code in a private Git repository,

35
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
giving you a version-controlled code repo,

36
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
CodeBuild, which is allowing you to build

37
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
and test your code in AWS in a serverless fashion,

38
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
CodeDeploy, which is allowing you

39
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,000
to deploy code onto servers,

40
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,000
so I bundle it both into the deployment

41
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:34,000
and the developer services

42
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,000
because they go along,

43
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,000
CodePipeline to do the orchestration

44
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
of a pipeline within AWS,

45
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
so from your code to your build, to your test,

46
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
your deployment, and your provisioning and so on,

47
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,000
CodeArtifacts to store software packages

48
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,000
and dependencies on AWS,

49
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,000
and then we have the CDK

50
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,000
to define your cloud infrastructure

51
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,000
using a programming language,

52
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,000
for example, JavaScript, TypeScript,

53
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Java, Python, et cetera.

54
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
And this gets compiled into a CloudFormation template.

55
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:03,000
So that's it.

56
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:04,000
I hope you liked it,

57
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:05,000
and I will see you in the next section.

